WEBVTT

1
00:00:07.456 --> 00:00:10.723
Marcus Bell: Hey Kyle, morning. How's everything down in Raleigh?

2
00:00:11.021 --> 00:00:17.958
Kyle Brennan: Morning, Marcus. We're good, busy. We just wrapped up our September numbers, and the phones were slammed with fall turf stuff.

3
00:00:19.234 --> 00:00:28.505
Marcus Bell: Yeah, that tracks with what I'm seeing. Call volume in the account is up about 12 percent month over month. Is that what you expected?

4
00:00:29.011 --> 00:00:38.225
Kyle Brennan: A little higher, honestly. We had two techs out with the flu for a week, so the office was juggling more calls than usual.

5
00:00:39.280 --> 00:00:45.921
Marcus Bell: Got it. Anything I can help with on the routing or the reporting side while things are hectic?

6
00:00:46.288 --> 00:00:53.991
Kyle Brennan: Actually, yeah. There's something I want to flag before I forget, and it's been bugging me for a few weeks.

7
00:00:55.423 --> 00:00:57.059
Marcus Bell: Go ahead, I'm listening.

8
00:00:58.393 --> 00:01:13.929
Kyle Brennan: It's the spam flags. Twice last month we had people call on a Sunday afternoon about a wasp nest, real customers, and Signal AI tagged them as spam. So they never showed up in the campaign reporting.

9
00:01:14.676 --> 00:01:20.416
Marcus Bell: Okay. And how did you end up finding out they were real calls?

10
00:01:20.999 --> 00:01:29.450
Kyle Brennan: Because they called back Monday and booked, and then my guy Nathan in the Durham office said he'd talked to them Sunday.

11
00:01:29.450 --> 00:01:33.675
Kyle Brennan: So I went digging and there they were, tagged as spam.

12
00:01:35.084 --> 00:01:39.469
Marcus Bell: Do you remember roughly which campaigns those came through on?

13
00:01:40.402 --> 00:01:52.711
Kyle Brennan: One was the weekend Google Ads push we run for the Raleigh and Cary area. That one's the problem, because the whole point of running ads Saturday and Sunday is to catch people when they're home.

14
00:01:53.607 --> 00:01:59.841
Marcus Bell: Right. So when those get marked spam, the weekend spend looks like it's not producing anything.

15
00:02:00.280 --> 00:02:11.548
Kyle Brennan: Exactly. The campaign looks dead when it's actually working. And I make budget calls off that reporting every month, so it's not just a cosmetic thing.

16
00:02:12.434 --> 00:02:26.653
Marcus Bell: That makes sense. Can you send me one or two of those call IDs? I want to pull the transcripts and the tags and take it back to the team, see what's triggering it.

17
00:02:27.270 --> 00:02:32.338
Kyle Brennan: Yeah, I'll email you a couple. There might be three total, I'll check.

18
00:02:32.856 --> 00:02:41.091
Marcus Bell: Perfect. I'll get that in front of the right folks and come back to you. I won't guess at a fix on the call.

19
00:02:41.476 --> 00:02:48.534
Kyle Brennan: Appreciate it. And while I've got you, one good thing: the summaries have been a hit with the front desk.

20
00:02:49.209 --> 00:02:50.923
Marcus Bell: Oh yeah? Tell me more.

21
00:02:52.128 --> 00:03:08.540
Kyle Brennan: So when someone's out or a callback gets handed to dispatch, they read the AI summary instead of listening to the whole recording. A couple of the newer folks said it's how they actually learned what a good call sounds like.

22
00:03:09.468 --> 00:03:16.881
Marcus Bell: That's good to hear. Are the new hires using them on their own, or is that something you're telling them to do?

23
00:03:17.900 --> 00:03:25.447
Kyle Brennan: On their own, mostly. I told them once and now they just do it. Saves me from re-explaining every call.

24
00:03:26.307 --> 00:03:32.672
Marcus Bell: Nice. I'll pass that along. Anything else on the account I should know about before we wrap?

25
00:03:34.142 --> 00:03:43.971
Kyle Brennan: Nothing major. We're still tracking our fair share of calls, attribution looks right, no complaints there. Just the spam thing and the usual end-of-month scramble.

26
00:03:45.394 --> 00:03:56.492
Marcus Bell: Great. So I'll look out for those call IDs, and I'll follow up once I've got an update. Anything you need from me on the renewal side before spring?

27
00:03:57.451 --> 00:04:00.316
Kyle Brennan: Not yet. We'll talk closer to it.

28
00:04:01.070 --> 00:04:06.088
Marcus Bell: Sounds good. Send those over whenever, and I'll take it from there. Talk soon.

29
00:04:06.445 --> 00:04:08.014
Kyle Brennan: Will do. Thanks, Marcus.
